sg-session (0.1.0-13) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Setup looks and works like Windows Setup: language and keyboard, Install
    now, license terms, installation type, account, "Where do you want to
    install", Ready, the Installing steps and a restart countdown -- full
    screen over a stained-glass backdrop on the installation media, or in a
    window from the live desktop. Our own drawing and icon.
  * A partitioner: drives, partitions and unallocated space with total size,
    free space and type; Refresh, Delete, Format and New (which makes an EFI
    system partition first on a disk without one), each applied when chosen,
    destructive ones behind a warning.
  * sg-install installs into a partition or unallocated space, not only over
    a whole disk: a file copy of the read-only live root into a fresh ext4,
    the kernels into the disk's EFI system partition -- or into a boot
    partition of its own beside another system's -- root pinned by PARTUUID,
    and a firmware boot entry. Other partitions and other systems' boot
    loaders are never touched; the boot menu is shown when Windows is there.
  * "Try Stained Glass OS": a live session (a passwordless live account made
    at each live boot in the volatile overlay; greetd and PAM sign it in) with
    "Install Stained Glass OS" on the desktop and in the Start menu. Setup's
    socket is now group sgsetup: the login screen and the live session.
  * Third-party drivers, Windows Update style: sg-drivers finds what a PC
    needs from Debian's non-free archive -- NVIDIA's driver as Debian's
    nvidia-detect chooses it (with the kernel and headers DKMS builds for),
    Broadcom's wl, graphics and Wi-Fi firmware -- and installs it. Setup's
    "Install third-party drivers for graphics and Wi-Fi (recommended)",
    checked by default, shows what it found; sg-drivers.service installs
    them at the first boot once the network is up, and retries. With the
    NVIDIA driver, nouveau is blacklisted on the kernel command line. With
    Secure Boot on, Setup makes the machine's own module signing key (root
    only), points DKMS at it and asks the firmware to enroll it, and shows
    the one-time password with the enrollment steps on its last page.

 -- Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>  Thu, 24 Sep 2026 18:40:11 -0700

sg-session (0.1.0-12) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Network settings: sg-netctl (CLI, the sg-netd socket service, and a bridge
    for Windows programs) over NetworkManager -- adapters, IPv4/IPv6 static or
    DHCP, DNS, Wi-Fi. Anyone with a Windows session may join Wi-Fi; only
    administrators change adapter settings. polkit rules hold NetworkManager
    to the same line. Gate: test/netctl-test.py.
  * sg-dc-provision pins a DHCP-given domain controller address as fixed.
  * sg-run-explorer starts the taskbar's network icon (sg-shell).

