sg-session (0.1.0-11) unstable; urgency=medium

  * User Group Policy de-tattoos too: sg_apply_user_policy applies the user's
    Registry.pol (HKCU) at login and deletes any value the user's policy set
    last time and no longer sets, so a restriction lifted in the directory
    lifts at the next login. State persists in the user's own home. The
    policy-value deletes are set -e safe (they run under sg-services-start /
    sg-run-explorer's set -eu). Gate: test/detattoo-test.sh (machine + user).

 -- Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>  Thu, 24 Sep 2026 15:58:34 -0700

sg-session (0.1.0-10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * sg_apply_policy de-tattoos: a registry value a policy set last time and no
    policy sets now is deleted on the next apply, so a policy removed from a
    GPO (or a local .reg dropped) stops applying, as Windows clears the
    managed policy branch each refresh. Only the exact values policy set are
    touched -- never a branch or a descriptor, so a program's own state under
    those keys is left alone. Gate: test/detattoo-test.sh.

 -- Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>  Thu, 24 Sep 2026 15:46:00 -0700

sg-session (0.1.0-9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Machine Group Policy from the domain: sg-gpo-machine fetches the
    computer's GPOs' Machine\Registry.pol from SYSVOL with the machine
    account (the GPOs Samba's get_gpo_list selects for it) into the machine
    policy directory; sg-gpupdate (gpupdate) fetches and applies it now, as
    SYSTEM; sg-gpupdate.timer refreshes it every 90 minutes and after boot.

